WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:07.440
How would you characterize, 
or describe your own world view?

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.140
I would call myself a social democrat.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.680
I think that is my commitment.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:19.000
I was brought up in a social democratic family,
and I go on with this.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:28.200
Social democracy has obviously some liberal ideas.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:34.880
But if you ask me in general terms to describe

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.660
I would say that I'm a social democrat.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:43.680
But there isn't much social democracy any more.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:51.260
No, what we see is that the hay day of social democracy was after the war.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.420
Those were really the most successful years.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:59.280
I think that what we see now is..

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.880
a decline of the organized labour.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.040
Because of the nature of the industries

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.900
the globalization, and so on.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:23.260
200 million people joined the work force in countries that weren't integrated in the global work force.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.880
And we are now in the midst of changes.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:32.700
So social democracy is challenged, 
both by the neoliberals

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.580
and by the people on the far left.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:41.840
I still think that the only sensible

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.700
world view in social terms

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.400
is social democracy

00:01:51.280 --> 00:02:00.680
Scandinavia and the Netherlands, when it was a social democracy, those are the places to look at.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.060
Why is there this ongoing struggle

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:11.160
for mankind to create a just society?

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:15.380
Because a just society is a good thing, 
so you try to bring in..

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.400
I mean, I see all the reasons to bring a just society.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:22.120
But why is it so difficult to succeed?

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.300
Because there are people who lose 
by having a just society.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:33.640
Many people, powerful people will lose a great deal
if there is a just society.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:38.680
The point is, are there enough people 
interested in justice.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:40.840
That's the problem. Just the reverse:

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:44.240
Why injustice? That's very easy to explain.

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.980
Why justice? is hard to explain.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:50.560
Why would people be motivated to 
bring about justice?

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:55.000
Enough people. And that's very hard, 
actually you don't have enough people

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:56.320
Why not?

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:01.220
Power is dispersed. To concentrate power is very hard.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:05.100
And when you concentrate power

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:10.140
you gain lots of things that you don't 
like to share with others.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:16.820
So the people that gain power, usually create 
oligarchies of some kind

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:23.040
And what used to be called 
'the iron law of the oligarchy'

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:33.440
I think there's a great deal to save from it,
and that's actually what you save in egalitarian

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.840
parties and egalitarian ideologies.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:40.840
Once they've got the power, their commitment is gone

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:46.000
the privilege is there, the power is there, 
why to share it with others?

